<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angoulême Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cathédrale Saint-Pierre d'Angoulême</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a> Cathedral, dedicated to Saint Peter (Saint-Pierre), is the cathedral of Angoulême in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Rebuilt in the first half of the 12th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> Girard II, it is a leading example of the Romanesque churches of south-western France roofed with a row of domes, and its west front is covered with sculpture. It was heavily restored by the architect Paul Abadie in the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angouleme-cathedral.jpg" alt="Angoulême Cathedral" width="320" height="452" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JLPC · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angoul%C3%AAme_16_Fa%C3%A7ade_cath%C3%A9drale_2014.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
